ARM64 LZ4 standalone regression test
==================================

This optional developer test cross-compiles the real LZ4 sources and emulates
AArch64/NEON instructions. It does NOT require an ARM64 host or device, and it
does not change the kernel configuration. It is deliberately not registered
in the normal native selftest run because it needs an emulator and a cross
compiler/linker.

Requirements:
  * Python >= 3.9 (run without -O; assertions are test checks)
  * pip packages unicorn==2.1.4 and pyelftools==0.32
  * Clang and LLD with AArch64, SCS and ThinLTO support

Example using a separately installed toolchain:
  python3 test_arm64.py --cc /path/to/clang

An alternative for freestanding checks only (NOT the release compiler) is
Zig 0.14.1's Clang 19.1.7, available as the ziglang==0.14.1 pip package:
  python3 test_arm64.py --cc '/path/to/zig cc'

By default artifacts live in an automatically removed temporary directory.
Use --output /path/outside/the/source/tree to retain objects and ELF files.
The --cc argument also accepts the CC environment variable. No GCC is used.

Two executables are tested: ordinary Clang and SCS+ThinLTO. The actual .S
file and C dispatch are used; only kernel headers, SIMD context protection,
CPU-part/FPSIMD queries, and memory primitives are replaced by small shims.
Memory-access hooks enforce input/output bounds and read-only compressed
input for nonoverlapping decoding. Callee-saved registers and x18 are checked.
The SCS executable must exercise shadow-stack writes, not merely accept a
compiler flag.

Coverage includes valid and malformed blocks, full/partial output, all short
match offsets, long lengths, unaligned buffers, multi-sequence blocks, C-tail
matches into the SIMD prefix, forbidden SIMD/FPSIMD-unavailable fallbacks,
DIP/overlapping-buffer fallbacks, small buffers, signed/size_t size guards,
and first/prefix/external-dictionary streaming blocks. Error offsets need
not be identical. Assembly rejects zero offsets even where upstream C may
accept them; any accepted random malformed block must match C's output.

The build also checks that STATIC, feature-off, and no-NEON objects do not
reference SIMD. It does not validate real kernel preemption, FPSIMD ownership,
module loading, the complete preboot wrapper, device boot or performance.
A full AOSP Clang r614150 kernel build and device tests remain necessary.
CONFIG_LZ4_ARM64_ACCEL is experimental and defaults to n.
